^^Inessive, elative, illative^^

It's time to get accuainted with an integral (and fun) part of the Finnish language, the *internal locative cases* e.g. how to say "inside", "from inside of" and into" something, also called "inessive, elative and illative". We already learned these for the different countries of the world, and now we will dive deeper!

This course will specifically target these three cases, because they actually used more often than simply when talking about something being inside of something else. Especially the elative, e.g. "from inside" case is required by many verbs. The next skill will target the *external locative cases*, where the same thing applies to the third one, e.g. "from outside". In English there are separate forms of the words "in/on, onto/into" but not for "from". Becasue of this, and because the "from" ending in Finnish is very often used for multiple purposes, we recommend focussing on understanding it in particular.

The suffixes we use are:
inside = *-ssa/-ssä*   "kirjassa" (in the book), "pöydässä" (inside the table)
into = *-een/seen/siin*   "kirjaan" (into the book), "pöytään" (into the table)
from inside = *-sta/-stä*   "kirjasta" (from/about the book), *pöydästä* (about the table, from inside the table)

As you can see, the *-sta/-stä* -form can be used to say 'from', but also 'about' something, e.g.

"Kirja*sta* tippuu kirjanmerkki" ('the bookmark falls from the book', and it was inside the book)
"Puhun kirja*sta*" ('I talk about the book')
or even:
"Opin sen kirja*sta*" ('I learned it from a book')

The *-sta/-stä* -form has many uses, and many of them can seem very silly, for example talking 'from inside of' a book, but this is not what is really meant. What is important is the verb used, e.g. if you use the verb *oppia* (to learn) then of course you're not coming out from a book to do it.

^What if I want to say something while being inside a house?^
The phrase *puhun sinulle talosta* can in fact mean both "I talk to you about the house" and "I talk to you from inside of the house" (e.g. talking through the window) but in this case the former would be preferred, because "about" is a more common meaning for the *-sta/-stä* -ending than "from".

^Verbs used with the *-sta/-stä* -form^

*löytää* (to find)
"löysin puhelimeni kaapista" (I found my phone from the closet)
In Finnish, you don't find something *in* the closet (unless you are inside it yourself), but *from* the closet.

*saada selvää* (to make sense of)
"En saanut viesti*stä* selvää" (I didn't make sense of the message)
E.g. I didn't "get any sense out of" the message.
